id: pm-gh1730
title: "GH-1730: the MCP flat-parameter parity spec compares the output-size token estimate of two concurrent reads that both write the context-usage ledger, so a Windows shard fails when one read waits on the ledger"
description: "GitHub issue #1730 (Nightly Validation, windows-latest / Node 24 shard 3/4, run 43589248357 on fcdd5f89d). tests/unit/mcp/read-tool-flat-parameters.spec.ts › 'applies flat intent and limit arguments exactly like nested options' runs the flat and the nested pm_next read concurrently (Promise.all) on one tracker and compared their whole context_intent blocks, which include estimated_tokens (325 vs 313 on the failing shard). Both context and next record context-usage serving rows under the ledger lock (recordContextUsageServing in src/sdk/query/next.ts and context.ts), so the rendered output of two concurrent reads can differ in runtime state (lock wait and signal-store warnings) while their parameter resolution is identical. estimated_tokens measures the rendered output, not the parameters. Root-cause comment already on the issue; family owner pm-2zjs0g is closed. Duplicate check: searched GH-1730, flat parameters, estimated_tokens concurrent reads; no open item owns the spec."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[6]: flaky-test,"gh:1730",mcp,nightly,test-reliability,windows
